1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,845
Session one.

2
00:00:01,125 --> 00:00:03,342
Network programming, from the socket up.

3
00:00:03,622 --> 00:00:05,138
By the end of this session,

4
00:00:05,138 --> 00:00:07,722
you will see that every web framework you have

5
00:00:07,722 --> 00:00:10,755
ever used is a thin wrapper
around seven system calls.

6
00:00:11,699 --> 00:00:13,572
Here is the core claim of the session.

7
00:00:13,852 --> 00:00:17,307
A TCP server is exactly seven system calls.

8
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:18,657
First, socket.

9
00:00:18,937 --> 00:00:20,583
Ask the kernel for an endpoint.

10
00:00:20,863 --> 00:00:25,390
AF_INET means IPv4, and SOCK_STREAM
means TCP, a byte stream.

11
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:26,339
Bind.

12
00:00:26,619 --> 00:00:30,219
Attach that socket to a port number, so
clients know where to find you.

13
00:00:30,566 --> 00:00:31,184
Listen.

14
00:00:31,464 --> 00:00:34,588
Tell the kernel to start accepting
connections on your behalf.

15
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:35,643
Accept.

16
00:00:35,923 --> 00:00:41,245
Block until a client connection arrives, and hand
it to you as a brand new file descriptor.

17
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:43,098
Read the bytes the client sent.

18
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:44,515
Write bytes back.

19
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:46,190
And close, when you are done.

20
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:50,730
Bind, listen and accept are the three
calls that only a server makes.

21
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:56,393
Express, Flask and Go's net/http are all
wrappers around this exact sequence.

22
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:57,876
A client is shorter.

23
00:00:58,156 --> 00:01:00,479
Socket, connect, read and write, close.

24
00:01:00,759 --> 00:01:02,066
Notice what is missing.

25
00:01:02,346 --> 00:01:05,335
A client never calls bind, and never calls listen.

26
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:08,906
Our course port for the demos is
two thousand twenty-six.

27
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:11,189
Let us look at the whole thing.

28
00:01:11,469 --> 00:01:12,656
About twenty lines.

29
00:01:12,999 --> 00:01:17,509
The program creates a socket, fills in an
address structure, binds, and listens.

30
00:01:17,789 --> 00:01:22,508
Then it loops forever: accept a client, read
once, write the same bytes back, close.

31
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:26,707
INADDR_ANY means listen on every
network interface of the machine.

32
00:01:27,333 --> 00:01:32,273
And htons converts the port number from the
host's byte order to network byte order.

33
00:01:32,553 --> 00:01:33,813
We will come back to that.

34
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:38,384
The number passed to listen is the backlog,
the depth of the accept queue.

35
00:01:38,664 --> 00:01:39,687
Also coming up.

36
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:41,367
One more point.

37
00:01:41,647 --> 00:01:43,596
One read is not a conversation.

38
00:01:43,876 --> 00:01:45,763
To make this server persistent,

39
00:01:45,763 --> 00:01:50,571
wrap read and write in a loop that continues
while read returns more than zero.

40
00:01:50,851 --> 00:01:54,541
When read returns zero, that is end
of file: the client closed.

41
00:01:55,466 --> 00:01:56,463
Next, ports.

42
00:01:56,743 --> 00:02:01,351
The rule is simple: everything below one
thousand twenty-four belongs to root.

43
00:02:02,399 --> 00:02:06,360
Below one thousand twenty-four are the
privileged, well-known ports.

44
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:07,959
Only root can bind them.

45
00:02:08,239 --> 00:02:18,825
Port 80 is HTTP, 443 is HTTPS, 25 is
SMTP, 21 is FTP, 110 is POP.

46
00:02:19,166 --> 00:02:20,858
Above that, anyone can bind.

47
00:02:21,138 --> 00:02:25,411
Three thousand for node, eighty-eighty
as an alternate HTTP port,

48
00:02:25,411 --> 00:02:29,684
sixty-three seventy-nine for Redis,
and twenty twenty-six for us.

49
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:31,171
Why does this matter?

50
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:34,681
If your server binds port eighty, it runs as root,

51
00:02:34,681 --> 00:02:38,427
so a bug in your request parser
becomes a root compromise.

52
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:40,432
Real deployments flip it around.

53
00:02:40,712 --> 00:02:43,789
The app binds eighty-eighty as
an unprivileged user,

54
00:02:43,789 --> 00:02:48,640
and a small front proxy that does almost
nothing owns eighty and four forty-three.

55
00:02:49,566 --> 00:02:52,496
A good way to learn what each
call does is to skip one.

56
00:02:52,776 --> 00:02:54,331
There are two classic mistakes.

57
00:02:55,366 --> 00:02:56,113
Case one.

58
00:02:56,393 --> 00:02:57,675
You never called bind.

59
00:02:57,955 --> 00:02:59,326
Nobody owns the port.

60
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:00,850
The client sends a SYN.

61
00:03:01,199 --> 00:03:03,919
The kernel answers immediately with an RST.

62
00:03:04,199 --> 00:03:06,116
The client sees connection refused.

63
00:03:06,396 --> 00:03:07,432
Fast, and loud.

64
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:10,466
Case two.

65
00:03:10,746 --> 00:03:13,294
You bind and listen, but never call accept.

66
00:03:13,574 --> 00:03:14,819
The client sends a SYN.

67
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:18,157
The kernel completes the three-way
handshake on your behalf.

68
00:03:18,499 --> 00:03:21,627
The connection is established, and
sits in the accept queue.

69
00:03:21,966 --> 00:03:23,839
But your process never picks it up.

70
00:03:24,119 --> 00:03:27,284
The client simply hangs until TCP times out.

71
00:03:27,564 --> 00:03:32,649
This looks exactly like an overloaded server,
which is why it is such a confusing bug.

72
00:03:33,566 --> 00:03:35,547
Let us look closer at the accept queue.

73
00:03:35,866 --> 00:03:40,389
The kernel keeps completed connections in a
queue until your code calls accept.

74
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:45,390
By the time accept returns, the connection has
already been established for a while.

75
00:03:45,733 --> 00:03:47,040
Three facts to remember.

76
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:49,972
What happens when the queue is
full is not portable.

77
00:03:50,252 --> 00:03:52,315
Some systems refuse, others hang.

78
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:55,763
Linux caps the number at net dot
core dot somaxconn,

79
00:03:55,763 --> 00:03:59,314
so the number in your code may not
be the number in effect.

80
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:02,186
And the backlog is the depth of the accept queue.

81
00:04:02,466 --> 00:04:05,486
It is not a limit on how many
connections you can have.

82
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:07,118
Signals.

83
00:04:07,398 --> 00:04:10,828
Most are harmless trivia, but one of
them will kill your server.

84
00:04:11,866 --> 00:04:13,375
SIGINT is signal two.

85
00:04:13,655 --> 00:04:15,282
It is what Ctrl C sends.

86
00:04:15,599 --> 00:04:17,986
SIGKILL is nine, and it cannot be caught.

87
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:19,720
SIGPIPE is thirteen.

88
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,930
If you write to a socket the
peer has already closed,

89
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:25,584
the default action is to terminate your process.

90
00:04:25,864 --> 00:04:27,379
No error, no exception.

91
00:04:27,659 --> 00:04:29,135
The process is just gone.

92
00:04:29,466 --> 00:04:31,701
SIGTERM is fifteen, the polite kill.

93
00:04:33,033 --> 00:04:38,429
In the demo, the client forces a reset using
SO_LINGER with on, and a timeout of zero.

94
00:04:38,709 --> 00:04:43,634
That sends an RST instead of a FIN, tears
the connection down immediately,

95
00:04:43,634 --> 00:04:46,429
discards unsent data, and skips TIME_WAIT.

96
00:04:46,766 --> 00:04:51,480
The real fix in servers is to ignore
SIGPIPE, or pass MSG_NOSIGNAL,

97
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:54,084
and handle the E PIPE error yourself.

98
00:04:55,033 --> 00:04:56,014
Now the client.

99
00:04:56,294 --> 00:05:00,119
It is shorter than the server, and the
interesting part is what is missing.

100
00:05:00,466 --> 00:05:01,616
Look up the host name.

101
00:05:01,896 --> 00:05:02,955
Create a socket.

102
00:05:03,235 --> 00:05:04,733
Fill in the address and port.

103
00:05:05,013 --> 00:05:07,322
Connect, which triggers the three-way handshake.

104
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:08,185
Write.

105
00:05:08,465 --> 00:05:09,102
Close.

106
00:05:09,433 --> 00:05:10,289
No bind.

107
00:05:10,569 --> 00:05:11,375
No listen.

108
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,571
So where does the client's port come from?

109
00:05:13,851 --> 00:05:18,097
The kernel picks a random high port at
connect time, from the ephemeral range.

110
00:05:18,377 --> 00:05:21,238
On Linux that is typically
thirty-two thousand seven

111
00:05:21,238 --> 00:05:24,704
hundred sixty-eight to sixty thousand
nine hundred ninety-nine.

112
00:05:24,984 --> 00:05:28,832
The server must bind a known port, because
clients need a fixed address.

113
00:05:29,112 --> 00:05:30,376
But the client does not.

114
00:05:30,656 --> 00:05:36,243
Ephemeral ports are finite, and TIME_WAIT holds
each for about sixty seconds after close,

115
00:05:36,243 --> 00:05:38,440
so busy clients really can run out.

116
00:05:39,366 --> 00:05:41,751
A quick but famous detail: byte order.

117
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,380
Port twenty twenty-six is hex zero seven EA.

118
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:51,481
A little-endian machine, like your laptop, stores
the low byte first: EA, then zero seven.

119
00:05:51,761 --> 00:05:55,909
But the network standard is big-
endian: zero seven, then EA.

120
00:05:56,233 --> 00:05:59,819
That is what htons does: host to
network, short, two bytes.

121
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:05,321
htonl is the four-byte version, and ntohs
and ntohl convert on the way back.

122
00:06:06,266 --> 00:06:11,083
One innocent function call in the client
hides an entire protocol: DNS.

123
00:06:11,433 --> 00:06:15,192
Step one, the file/etc/hosts,
before any network traffic.

124
00:06:15,472 --> 00:06:20,904
That is why editing it overrides everything, and
why a stale entry is so hard to debug.

125
00:06:21,233 --> 00:06:27,062
Step two, a UDP query to your resolver, which
is listed in etc/resolv dot conf.

126
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:29,569
The resolver recurses on your behalf.

127
00:06:29,849 --> 00:06:31,373
First it asks a root server.

128
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:34,997
Then the top-level domain server, for example.com.

129
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:39,094
And finally the authoritative server
that actually knows the answer.

130
00:06:39,433 --> 00:06:43,046
The answer is cached for its TTL,
so it is fast when warm.

131
00:06:43,366 --> 00:06:44,458
But the call blocks.

132
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:48,398
A cold lookup can take seconds, and you
do not control the timeout.

133
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:50,729
In real code, use getaddrinfo.

134
00:06:51,009 --> 00:06:54,188
gethostbyname is IPv4 only and not thread safe.

135
00:06:55,133 --> 00:06:58,111
Before writing any code, you
already own three clients.

136
00:06:59,133 --> 00:06:59,765
telnet.

137
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:03,821
If a protocol is text, your keyboard
is an implementation of it.

138
00:07:04,101 --> 00:07:09,691
Type GET/HTTP one point one, a Host header,
and a blank line, and Google answers.

139
00:07:10,033 --> 00:07:12,818
openssl s_client, for anything encrypted,

140
00:07:12,818 --> 00:07:16,282
because telnet is useless once
TLS gets in the way.

141
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:18,243
And netcat for everything else.

142
00:07:18,523 --> 00:07:21,855
nc localhost 2026 is a client.

143
00:07:22,135 --> 00:07:24,772
nc -l 9000 is a tiny server.

144
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:28,341
The response is a three hundred
one, moved permanently,

145
00:07:28,341 --> 00:07:32,289
cacheable for two million five hundred
ninety-two thousand seconds.

146
00:07:32,569 --> 00:07:33,746
That is thirty days.

147
00:07:34,026 --> 00:07:39,100
CRLF line endings, and the blank line that
ends the headers are mandatory.

148
00:07:40,633 --> 00:07:42,530
And where are we on the OSI stack?

149
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:45,212
We have been writing layer seven this whole time.

150
00:07:45,492 --> 00:07:47,764
Ports belong to layer four, transport.

151
00:07:48,044 --> 00:07:50,076
IP addresses belong to layer three.

152
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,367
Our server handles one client at a time.

153
00:07:53,647 --> 00:07:54,908
How do we handle many?

154
00:07:55,233 --> 00:07:56,067
Two answers.

155
00:07:56,347 --> 00:07:58,549
One process per connection, with fork.

156
00:07:58,829 --> 00:08:03,883
Dead simple, the operating system schedules it,
and a crash is contained to one child.

157
00:08:04,163 --> 00:08:07,401
Fine for hundreds of clients,
hopeless at ten thousand.

158
00:08:07,733 --> 00:08:11,448
Or one process with many sockets,
using select or epoll.

159
00:08:11,728 --> 00:08:15,237
Ask the kernel which sockets are
ready, and handle only those.

160
00:08:15,517 --> 00:08:18,256
No context switching, no per-client memory.

161
00:08:18,536 --> 00:08:21,676
Harder to write, and one slow
handler blocks everything.

162
00:08:21,956 --> 00:08:23,877
This is what nginx and Node do.

163
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:27,445
Fork returns twice.

164
00:08:27,725 --> 00:08:29,277
Zero means you are the child.

165
00:08:29,557 --> 00:08:31,389
Handle this client, then exit.

166
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:36,871
A positive value means you are the parent, and
the value is the child's process ID.

167
00:08:37,151 --> 00:08:40,668
Close your copy of the file
descriptor and go back to accept.

168
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,902
Minus one means the fork failed.

169
00:08:43,182 --> 00:08:44,804
Out of processes or memory.

170
00:08:45,084 --> 00:08:46,485
Almost nobody checks.

171
00:08:48,033 --> 00:08:49,460
select versus epoll.

172
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:51,659
Select is portable but O of n.

173
00:08:51,939 --> 00:08:55,631
You rebuild the set every call, the
kernel walks all of them,

174
00:08:55,631 --> 00:08:59,627
and FD_SETSIZE caps you near one
thousand twenty-four descriptors.

175
00:08:59,907 --> 00:09:02,380
epoll is Linux only and O of ready.

176
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:07,586
You register interest once, and the kernel
returns only the descriptors that changed.

177
00:09:07,866 --> 00:09:11,798
BSD and macOS have kqueue, Windows has IOCP.

178
00:09:12,078 --> 00:09:14,057
nginx and Node are built on this.

179
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:16,689
Some limits to know.

180
00:09:16,969 --> 00:09:19,836
Six was Chrome's old cap on connections per host.

181
00:09:20,116 --> 00:09:22,002
Requests seven to ten simply queue.

182
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:26,093
ulimit -n is the hard ceiling on
open file descriptors.

183
00:09:26,373 --> 00:09:29,661
Sockets count, and it is often one
thousand twenty-four.

184
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,057
A connection pool opens
connections once and reuses them,

185
00:09:34,057 --> 00:09:37,759
skipping the TCP and TLS
handshakes after the first.

186
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:41,350
And SO_REUSEADDR is the option
you will actually use.

187
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:45,552
Without it, restarting your server fails
with address already in use,

188
00:09:45,552 --> 00:09:47,769
while the old socket sits in TIME_WAIT.

189
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:52,443
Promiscuous mode lets a network card see
traffic that is not addressed to it.

190
00:09:52,723 --> 00:09:56,263
That is how sniffers work, and why
plaintext protocols died.

191
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:02,341
Finally, to see the actual bytes,
use tcpdump or Wireshark.

192
00:10:02,621 --> 00:10:06,936
Capture to a file, then read it back as
ASCII, or as hex and ASCII.

193
00:10:07,866 --> 00:10:08,698
TLS.

194
00:10:08,978 --> 00:10:13,678
The key sentence is: SSL secures TCP, not HTTP.

195
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,771
TLS sits underneath the application protocol, so
the same machinery protects HTTP, mail,

196
00:10:20,771 --> 00:10:23,234
databases, anything on a socket.

197
00:10:23,566 --> 00:10:24,474
How it works.

198
00:10:24,754 --> 00:10:28,341
First, asymmetric cryptography
agrees a shared secret.

199
00:10:28,621 --> 00:10:33,390
Then symmetric cryptography encrypts the data,
because it is dramatically faster.

200
00:10:33,733 --> 00:10:36,642
Second, a certificate binds a key to an identity.

201
00:10:36,922 --> 00:10:38,976
The CA chain is why you believe it.

202
00:10:39,256 --> 00:10:42,385
And certificates expire, which is
the three AM outage.

203
00:10:42,733 --> 00:10:46,643
Third, certificate pinning removes the
CA from the trust equation.

204
00:10:46,923 --> 00:10:50,027
Common in mobile apps, trust
this exact certificate.

205
00:10:50,966 --> 00:10:52,370
Now protocol design.

206
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:55,101
The single most important decision is framing.

207
00:10:55,433 --> 00:10:58,439
TCP gives you a byte stream, not messages.

208
00:10:58,719 --> 00:11:03,548
When you write two messages, the receiver may read
them glued together, or split in half.

209
00:11:03,828 --> 00:11:06,436
So how does the receiver know
where one message ends?

210
00:11:07,466 --> 00:11:08,307
Option one.

211
00:11:08,587 --> 00:11:09,450
Fixed length.

212
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:12,024
Every message is exactly N bytes.

213
00:11:12,304 --> 00:11:16,187
Trivial to parse, but you pay in
padding, and you can never change N.

214
00:11:16,533 --> 00:11:17,388
Option two.

215
00:11:17,668 --> 00:11:18,370
Delimited.

216
00:11:18,650 --> 00:11:22,221
Read until a sentinel, like the
blank line in HTTP.

217
00:11:22,501 --> 00:11:26,768
Simple, until the delimiter shows up in your
data, and then you need escaping.

218
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:27,986
Option three.

219
00:11:28,266 --> 00:11:29,309
Length prefix.

220
00:11:29,589 --> 00:11:32,222
Send the size, then the bytes,
like Content-Length.

221
00:11:32,502 --> 00:11:34,100
Robust and binary safe.

222
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:36,646
This is what most modern protocols do.

223
00:11:37,566 --> 00:11:40,076
Some encodings you will meet in real systems.

224
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:41,811
Binary coded decimal.

225
00:11:42,091 --> 00:11:44,541
Four bits, one nibble, per decimal digit.

226
00:11:44,821 --> 00:11:49,890
Ninety-eight thousand seven hundred sixty-
five becomes one-zero-zero-one,

227
00:11:49,890 --> 00:11:51,945
one-zero-zero-zero, and so on.

228
00:11:52,225 --> 00:11:57,066
In hex it reads nine eight, seven six, five
F, where F pads the odd nibble.

229
00:11:57,346 --> 00:11:58,841
Digits just show up in hex.

230
00:11:59,121 --> 00:12:05,155
Half the size of ASCII, and still used in SIM
cards, SMS and card transactions.

231
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:08,790
TLV: type, length, value.

232
00:12:09,070 --> 00:12:11,582
It is ASN.1 you can write on a napkin.

233
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:14,039
The magic is that it is self-describing.

234
00:12:14,319 --> 00:12:17,600
A parser that does not know a type can
skip it using the length.

235
00:12:17,933 --> 00:12:20,190
So new fields do not break old readers.

236
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:22,101
That is forward compatibility.

237
00:12:22,433 --> 00:12:28,695
TLV is used by TLS extensions, ISO eighty-
five eighty-three, and protobuf.

238
00:12:30,233 --> 00:12:32,399
ASN.1, from nineteen eighty-four.

239
00:12:32,679 --> 00:12:36,408
Write the grammar once, and the encoder
and decoder are generated.

240
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:42,544
It still sits under X five oh nine
certificates, LDAP, SNMP and the telecom stack.

241
00:12:42,866 --> 00:12:44,020
Hex is for reading.

242
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,923
Two characters per byte, as in tcpdump -capital X.

243
00:12:48,266 --> 00:12:54,413
Base sixty-four is for transport over text-
only channels: basic auth, JWTs,

244
00:12:54,413 --> 00:12:56,758
email attachments, data URIs.

245
00:12:57,099 --> 00:12:58,833
It costs thirty-three percent more.

246
00:12:59,113 --> 00:13:01,024
Three bytes become four characters.

247
00:13:01,304 --> 00:13:02,713
A wrapper, not a design.

248
00:13:03,633 --> 00:13:06,107
Finally, RPC and gRPC.

249
00:13:06,433 --> 00:13:08,962
RPC is framing plus a function name.

250
00:13:09,242 --> 00:13:13,215
First, marshal: turn the function name
and its arguments into bytes.

251
00:13:13,533 --> 00:13:14,755
Then frame and send them.

252
00:13:15,099 --> 00:13:16,965
But the network is not a function call.

253
00:13:17,245 --> 00:13:21,563
Calls fail halfway, time out with the work
possibly done, and get retried.

254
00:13:21,899 --> 00:13:24,561
Then the server unmarshals and runs the function.

255
00:13:24,899 --> 00:13:28,270
gRPC is RPC with protobuf underneath.

256
00:13:28,550 --> 00:13:33,483
You write a dot proto schema first, and generate
client and server code in any language.

257
00:13:33,833 --> 00:13:37,867
On the wire it is TLV, so new fields
do not break old clients.

258
00:13:38,199 --> 00:13:42,187
It runs on HTTP two, which is
binary and multiplexed.

259
00:13:42,533 --> 00:13:44,929
The cost is that you cannot read it with curl.

260
00:13:45,209 --> 00:13:46,984
Speed bought with debuggability.

261
00:13:47,264 --> 00:13:49,877
Faster than JSON, easier than ASN.1.

262
00:13:50,799 --> 00:13:54,161
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session one.

263
00:14:14,366 --> 00:14:15,458
That is session one.

264
00:14:15,738 --> 00:14:19,281
Next up, session two: the eagle-eye
view of the network stack.

